---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const DEMO_URL = "/demo/";
const REPO_URL = "https://github.com/5uper0/parley";

const wrap = "mx-auto w-full max-w-[880px] px-6 md:px-10";

const sectionLabel =
  "font-mono text-[11.5px] font-semibold tracking-[0.08em] text-subtle uppercase";

const card = "rounded-[14px] border border-hairline bg-surface";

const deviceBlock =
  "overflow-x-auto rounded-[10px] border border-hairline bg-bg px-[18px] py-4 font-mono text-[12.5px] leading-[1.9] text-muted";

const link =
  "text-indigo-text underline decoration-hairline underline-offset-2 transition-colors hover:decoration-current";

const code =
  "rounded-[5px] border border-hairline bg-surface px-[5px] py-px font-mono text-[0.92em] text-ink";

const btnPrimary =
  "inline-flex min-h-12 items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-indigo px-7 py-3.5 text-[15px] font-[640] text-white shadow-[inset_0_1px_0_rgba(255,255,255,.10)] transition-all duration-200 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.16,1,0.3,1)] hover:-translate-y-px hover:brightness-[1.06] active:translate-y-0 active:scale-[0.98]";

const btnGhost =
  "group inline-flex min-h-12 items-center gap-1.5 text-[15px] font-medium text-muted transition-colors hover:text-ink";

const contrast = [
  {
    k: "Where it lives",
    prompt: "In the system prompt, as natural language the model is asked to honour.",
    parley: "In a PreferenceSheet, as a Python callable the engine runs on every option.",
  },
  {
    k: "Who decides it was crossed",
    prompt: "The model, in context, on every turn, under whatever pressure the other side applies.",
    parley: "PreferenceSheet.evaluate. The predicate returns True or the option is infeasible.",
  },
  {
    k: "Can a strong enough offer override it",
    prompt: "Yes. A constraint in a prompt competes with everything else in the prompt.",
    parley: "No. Scores are only compared among options that already cleared every red line.",
  },
  {
    k: "What the other side learns",
    prompt: "Whatever the model says while explaining its refusal, which may be the constraint itself.",
    parley: "The word \"red-line\". Not the name of the constraint and not the sheet.",
  },
  {
    k: "How the owner checks afterwards",
    prompt: "Read the chat log and hope the model's account of itself is accurate.",
    parley: "verify_non_betrayal(sheet, decision) re-runs the sheet locally and returns a boolean.",
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "What exactly counts as crossing a red line?",
    a: "Any return value other than the literal True. The predicate is called with the option; PreferenceSheet.evaluate lists a constraint as violated unless predicate(option) is True. A truthy value such as 1 or a non-empty string still counts as crossed, and so do False and None. The check fails closed by design.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can a red line be weighted, softened, or traded against a very high score?",
    a: "No. Hard constraints and soft utility are separate fields on the sheet and are combined nowhere. run_consensus only computes scores for options where every agent returned acceptable, so an infeasible option never enters the comparison, whatever its scores would have been.",
  },
  {
    q: "What does the coordinator see when my red line fires?",
    a: "A Verdict with acceptable set to False, your soft score, and the reason \"red-line\". The names of the crossed constraints are computed inside evaluate and kept in an Evaluation object that never leaves your agent. The score itself is public in the transcript; SECURITY.md lists that as a known limit.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does this need an LLM at all?",
    a: "No. The core is standard-library Python with no dependencies, and a red line is a plain function. A language model can sit upstream, in parley.elicit, to draft a sheet from a participant's answers, but it only proposes plain data that is validated against a fixed schema and then confirmed by the participant. The enforcement path never consults a model.",
  },
];

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "TechArticle",
      "@id": "https://parleyprotocol.com/llm-agent-red-lines/#article",
      headline: "Red lines for LLM agents: enforced in code, not in the prompt",
      description:
        "What a red line is in Parley: a code predicate that rejects an option outright and is never traded away as soft utility. Why that differs from telling a model to never do X, and how the owner verifies it afterwards.",
      url: "https://parleyprotocol.com/llm-agent-red-lines/",
      inLanguage: "en",
      author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Parley", url: "https://parleyprotocol.com/" },
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Parley", url: "https://parleyprotocol.com/" },
      about: {
        "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
        name: "Parley",
        url: "https://parleyprotocol.com/",
        codeRepository: REPO_URL,
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://parleyprotocol.com/llm-agent-red-lines/#faq",
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
      })),
    },
  ],
});
---

<Layout
  title="Red lines for LLM agents, enforced in code — Parley"
  description="A red line in Parley is a code predicate that rejects an option outright, never a soft preference a model can be argued out of. How HardConstraint and PreferenceSheet.evaluate enforce it, and how the owner checks afterwards."
  path="/llm-agent-red-lines/"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={jsonLd} />

  <a
    href="#main"
    class="sr-only focus:not-sr-only focus:absolute focus:top-3 focus:left-3 focus:z-50 focus:rounded-full focus:bg-indigo focus:px-5 focus:py-3.5 focus:text-sm focus:font-semibold focus:text-white"
  >
    Skip to content
  </a>

  <div class="flex min-h-screen flex-col">
    <div class={wrap}>
      <Header />
    </div>

    <main id="main" class="flex-1 pb-20">
      <!-- Hero -->
      <section class={wrap}>
        <p class="mb-6 flex items-center gap-2.5 font-mono text-xs tracking-[1.4px] whitespace-nowrap text-muted uppercase">
          Guide
          <span class="h-px min-w-6 flex-1 bg-hairline" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="font-display text-[clamp(2.1rem,5.5vw,3.3rem)] leading-[1.06] tracking-[-0.028em] text-ink">
          A red line an LLM can be talked out of is not a red line.
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 max-w-[58ch] text-[clamp(1rem,1.5vw,1.12rem)] leading-[1.55] text-muted">
          "Never agree to more than 700" in a system prompt is a request. The model weighs
          it against everything else in the context, including the other side's best
          argument. In Parley a red line is a predicate the engine runs on every option,
          and an option that fails it cannot win. This page shows the mechanism, the
          fields it touches, and the check the owner runs afterwards.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[13px] text-subtle">
          Every mechanism named here lives in{" "}
          <code class={code}>parley/preferences.py</code> and{" "}
          <code class={code}>parley/agent.py</code> in the{" "}
          <a href={REPO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class={link}>
            source repository
          </a>. Companion guides:{" "}
          <a href="/fair-multi-agent-negotiation/" class={link}>fair multi-agent negotiation</a>,{" "}
          <a href="/mcp-multi-party-decisions/" class={link}>running Parley as an MCP tool</a>,{" "}
          <a href="/verify-ai-agent-decision/" class={link}>verifying a decision receipt</a>{" "}
          and{" "}
          <a href="/fair-group-decision-private-limits/" class={link}>a fair group decision with private limits</a>.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-10 h-px w-full bg-redline/60" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      </section>

      <!-- Definition -->
      <section class={`${wrap} mt-14`}>
        <h2 class={sectionLabel}>What a red line is, in one dataclass</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px]/[25px] text-muted">
          A hard constraint is a name and a predicate. The name is for the owner. The
          predicate is a function of the option, and the contract is strict: the option is
          acceptable only if the predicate returns the literal <code class={code}>True</code>.
          Any other return value, truthy or not, crosses it.
        </p>
        <pre class={`${deviceBlock} mt-5 whitespace-pre`}><code>{`@dataclass(frozen=True)
class HardConstraint:
    name: str
    predicate: Callable[[Any], bool]

# a red line: code, not a preference
HardConstraint("budget", lambda o: o["cost"] <= 700)`}</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px]/[25px] text-muted">
          A <code class={code}>PreferenceSheet</code> holds a list of these under
          <code class={code}>hard</code>, and separately an optional
          <code class={code}>utility</code> function that returns a soft score. The two
          fields are never mixed. That separation is the whole design: soft utility ranks
          options that already clear every red line, and nothing else.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- evaluate -->
      <section class={`${wrap} mt-14`}>
        <h2 class={sectionLabel}>What PreferenceSheet.evaluate does with it</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px]/[25px] text-muted">
          <code class={code}>evaluate(option)</code> returns an
          <code class={code}>Evaluation</code> with three fields. It runs every predicate,
          collects the names of the ones that did not return <code class={code}>True</code>,
          and sets <code class={code}>feasible</code> only when that list is empty. The
          soft score is computed regardless: the owner's utility clamped to [0, 1], or a
          neutral 0.5 when no utility was given. A utility that returns NaN raises
          <code class={code}>UtilityError</code>, since that is a fault in the sheet, not in
          the option.
        </p>
        <div class={`${deviceBlock} mt-5`} aria-label="The shape of an Evaluation">
          <span class="text-muted">Evaluation(</span><br />
          &nbsp;&nbsp;feasible=<span class="text-redline-text">False</span>, <span class="text-muted"># every predicate returned True?</span><br />
          &nbsp;&nbsp;violated=<span class="text-masked">["budget"]</span>, <span class="text-muted"># stays private to the owner</span><br />
          &nbsp;&nbsp;score=0.1, <span class="text-muted"># soft utility in [0, 1]</span><br />
          <span class="text-muted">)</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px]/[25px] text-muted">
          The <code class={code}>violated</code> list is where the constraint names end up,
          and it is the last place they exist. The next layer discards it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Masking -->
      <section class={`${wrap} mt-14`}>
        <h2 class={sectionLabel}>What leaves the agent</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px]/[25px] text-muted">
          <code class={code}>Agent.consider(option)</code> calls
          <code class={code}>evaluate</code> and returns a <code class={code}>Verdict</code>.
          The verdict carries <code class={code}>acceptable</code>, the score, and a
          <code class={code}>reason</code> that is the string <code class={code}>"ok"</code>
          when the option is feasible and <code class={code}>"red-line"</code> when it is
          not. The sheet is never handed to the coordinator, and the coordinator is untrusted
          by construction: it sees that a line was crossed, never which one.
        </p>
        <pre class={`${deviceBlock} mt-5 whitespace-pre`}><code>{`def consider(self, option):
    ev = self.sheet.evaluate(option)
    return Verdict(
        owner=self.owner,
        acceptable=ev.feasible,
        score=ev.score,
        reason="ok" if ev.feasible else "red-line",
    )`}</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px]/[25px] text-muted">
          Downstream, <code class={code}>run_consensus</code> treats a single
          <code class={code}>acceptable=False</code> as a veto: an option is a candidate
          only when every agent accepted it, and scores are compared among candidates only.
          A red line is therefore never outvoted and never outbid.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Contrast -->
      <section class={`${wrap} mt-14`}>
        <h2 class={sectionLabel}>Prompted constraint versus enforced red line</h2>
        <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-[14px] border border-hairline bg-surface">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[680px] border-collapse text-left">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b border-hairline">
                <th scope="col" class="px-5 py-3.5 font-mono text-[11.5px] tracking-[0.06em] text-subtle uppercase"></th>
                <th scope="col" class="px-5 py-3.5 text-[13px] font-semibold text-muted">"Never do X" in a prompt</th>
                <th scope="col" class="px-5 py-3.5 text-[13px] font-semibold text-indigo-text">A Parley red line</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                contrast.map((r) => (
                  <tr class="border-b border-hairline last:border-0">
                    <th
                      scope="row"
                      class="px-5 py-3.5 align-top font-mono text-[11.5px] tracking-[0.04em] text-subtle uppercase"
                    >
                      {r.k}
                    </th>
                    <td class="px-5 py-3.5 align-top text-[14px]/[22px] text-muted">{r.prompt}</td>
                    <td class="px-5 py-3.5 align-top text-[14px]/[22px] text-ink">{r.parley}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Verification -->
      <section class={`${wrap} mt-14`}>
        <h2 class={sectionLabel}>Checking it held, without showing anyone the sheet</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px]/[25px] text-muted">
          After a decision, each owner runs
          <code class={code}>transcript.verify_non_betrayal(sheet, decision)</code> on their
          own machine. It re-evaluates the private sheet against the decision and returns
          <code class={code}>feasible</code>. Nothing is transmitted; the sheet never leaves.
          A deadlock, where no option cleared every red line, returns
          <code class={code}>True</code> for everyone, since a deadlock forces nothing on
          anyone. The transcript itself hashes with SHA-256 over its canonical JSON, so an
          edit to the record after the fact changes the digest.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-[62ch] text-[15px]/[25px] text-muted">
          From an MCP host such as Claude or Cursor, the same replay is the
          <code class={code}>parley_check_party</code> tool, which takes one party's red
          lines and a decision. In that mode the host holds every party's spec in one call,
          so there is no privacy between parties; what still holds is the deterministic
          rejection.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Limits -->
      <section class={`${wrap} mt-14`}>
        <h2 class={sectionLabel}>What a red line does not do</h2>
        <div class={`${card} mt-5 px-5 py-[18px]`}>
          <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3 text-[14.5px]/[23px] text-muted">
            <li>
              <strong class="text-ink">It does not read minds.</strong> Non-betrayal is
              provable with respect to the predicates the owner actually wrote. A
              constraint that was meant but not encoded is not enforced, and Parley does
              not judge whether a sheet captures what someone intended.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-ink">It does not hide the soft score.</strong> The score
              is public in the transcript. An untrusted coordinator can infer preference
              ordering and, over enough options, the shape of the feasible region.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-ink">It does not sign anything by default.</strong> The
              transcript digest is an unsigned SHA-256. Ed25519-signed verdicts are an
              optional extra, and SECURITY.md describes even those as tamper-evidence
              rather than proof of who signed.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong class="text-ink">A model never writes the predicate.</strong>
              <code class={code}>parley.elicit</code> can draft a sheet from a participant's
              own answers, but the language model sits behind an injectable callable and
              can only propose plain data. Every proposed item is validated against the
              declarative <code class={code}>Constraint</code> schema in
              <code class={code}>spec.py</code>, never evaluated as code, and nothing is
              binding until the participant confirms every hard constraint by id.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class={`${wrap} mt-14`}>
        <h2 class={sectionLabel}>Questions this page gets asked</h2>
        <div class="mt-5 flex flex-col gap-2.5">
          {
            faqs.map((f) => (
              <details class={`${card} px-5 py-4`}>
                <summary class="cursor-pointer text-[15px]/[24px] font-semibold text-ink marker:text-subtle">
                  {f.q}
                </summary>
                <p class="mt-3 max-w-[62ch] text-[14.5px]/[23px] text-muted">{f.a}</p>
              </details>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- CTA -->
      <section class={`${wrap} mt-14`}>
        <div class="h-px w-full bg-redline/60" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <h2 class="mt-8 font-display text-[26px] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">
          Watch a red line block an option, then read the predicate that did it.
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-[52ch] text-[15px]/[25px] text-muted">
          The demo replays pre-computed runs of the same engine, no signup. The repository is
          Apache-2.0; <code class={code}>preferences.py</code> is under sixty lines.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-6">
          <a href={DEMO_URL} class={btnPrimary}>Open the demo</a>
          <a href={REPO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class={btnGhost}>
            Read the source
            <span
              class="inline-block transition-transform duration-200 ease-[cubic-bezier(0.16,1,0.3,1)] group-hover:translate-x-[3px]"
              aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span
            >
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-8 text-[13.5px] text-subtle">
          <a href="/" class={link}>Back to the overview</a> &middot;{" "}
          <a href="/fair-multi-agent-negotiation/" class={link}>Next: how the whole negotiation stays fair</a> &middot;{" "}
          <a href="/mcp-multi-party-decisions/" class={link}>Setup: Parley as an MCP tool</a> &middot;{" "}
          <a href="/verify-ai-agent-decision/" class={link}>Then: verify the receipt afterwards</a> &middot;{" "}
          <a href="/fair-group-decision-private-limits/" class={link}>Worked case: a group decision with private limits</a>
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <Footer />
  </div>
</Layout>
